From: Waltteri Hooli <1420194+wahooli@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: qcom: tear the root complex down for suspend-to-idle

dw_pcie_suspend_noirq() tears a root complex down only when the D3cold
walk allows it, and on a devicetree system it never does: the root port
has no platform power management and stays in D0. So both RCs stay up
through s2idle with their GDSCs, PHYs, XO-fed aux clocks and a 1 kBps
memory vote in the RPMh sleep set, and the SoC never reaches CX collapse,
AOSS sleep or DDR low power.

Tear the RC down for s2idle regardless. The endpoints are power cycled
through pwrctrl on the way and have to cope: ath11k already reloads its
firmware on every resume, and xhci-pci-renesas does with the next patch.

Measured on the AYANEO Pocket VERT (SM8475P): qcom_stats aosd/cxsd/ddr
stayed at 0 through every s2idle; with both RCs down they covered 120.5
of 121.8 s asleep. Switchable at runtime through the s2idle_teardown
module parameter.

Deep suspend (PSCI SYSTEM_SUSPEND, the AYN Odin 3) has the same problem,
worse: an RC whose bandwidth comes from its OPP table (SM8750) has no
icc_mem path, and the suspend OPP is skipped for S2RAM, so the full OPP
vote stays. The Odin 3 held DDR at 1708 MHz with aosd/cxsd/ddr at 0
through every deep sleep. The deep_teardown parameter does the same
teardown there, on by default.

On SM8750 the teardown lets the APSS release every system NoC vote, and
the wake then hangs unless the SoCCP is parked (9019-9021) and the PCIe
GDSCs really collapse (upstream's PWRSTS_OFF_ON).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware.h
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware.h
@@ -471,6 +471,7 @@
 	bool			native_ecam;
 	bool                    skip_l23_ready;
 	bool			skip_pwrctrl_off;
+	bool			force_teardown;
 };
 
 struct dw_pcie_ep_ops {
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-designware-host.c
@@ -1231,7 +1231,9 @@
 	if (!dw_pcie_link_up(pci))
 		goto stop_link;
 
-	if (!pci_host_common_d3cold_possible(pci->pp.bridge, &pme_capable))
+	/* force_teardown: the host driver has its endpoints cope with power loss. */
+	if (!pci_host_common_d3cold_possible(pci->pp.bridge, &pme_capable) &&
+	    !pci->pp.force_teardown)
 		return 0;
 
 	if (pci->pp.ops->pme_turn_off) {
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -2408,6 +2408,28 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/*
+ * s2idle_teardown: tear the RC down for suspend-to-idle even when the D3cold
+ * walk refuses, which on a devicetree system it always does. Left up, the RC
+ * keeps its GDSC, PHY, aux clock and a sleep-set memory vote, and the SoC
+ * never reaches CX collapse or DDR low power. The endpoints are power cycled
+ * through pwrctrl.
+ */
+static bool s2idle_teardown = true;
+module_param(s2idle_teardown, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(s2idle_teardown,
+		 "s2idle: tear the RC down at suspend even when the D3cold walk refuses (default 1)");
+
+/*
+ * deep_teardown: the same for deep suspend. An RC whose bandwidth comes from
+ * its OPP table (SM8750) otherwise keeps the full OPP vote: icc_mem is NULL
+ * and the suspend OPP is skipped for S2RAM.
+ */
+static bool deep_teardown = true;
+module_param(deep_teardown, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(deep_teardown,
+		 "deep: tear the RC down at suspend even when the D3cold walk refuses (default 1)");
+
 static int qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct qcom_pcie *pcie;
@@ -2417,7 +2439,11 @@
 	if (!pcie)
 		return 0;

+	pcie->pci->pp.force_teardown =
+		(s2idle_teardown && pm_suspend_target_state == PM_SUSPEND_TO_IDLE) ||
+		(deep_teardown && pm_suspend_target_state == PM_SUSPEND_MEM);
 	ret = dw_pcie_suspend_noirq(pcie->pci);
+	pcie->pci->pp.force_teardown = false;
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
